---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const dsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'Array / list — ordered shelf', note: 'Items in a row, numbered from 0. Instant access to any position (random access); inserting in the middle means shuffling everything after it.' },
    { math: 'Hash table — the phone book', note: 'A key hashed into a slot: average instant lookup by name, not position. The workhorse behind dictionaries, caches, and database indexes.' },
    { math: 'Stack — last in, first out', note: 'Push and pop from one end. The "undo" pile, and the mechanism tracking function calls in every running program.' },
    { math: 'Queue — first in, first out', note: 'The fair line. Print jobs, message systems, and breadth-first search all run on queues.' },
    { math: 'Tree — hierarchy with fast search', note: 'A branching structure: file systems, JSON, and binary search trees that find items in log n steps. Balanced trees (AVL, B-tree) keep them fast even under abuse.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/data-structures">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Data structure = container + trade-off.</strong> Every structure makes some
    operations instant and others expensive; choosing one is choosing which question you need to
    be fast. The classic exam question — "array or hash table?" — is really "random access by
    position, or lookup by name?" Know the operations you need, and the structure picks itself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five containers everything else is built from" widget="equationStep" config={dsCfg} />

  <h2>The cost table every programmer carries</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Array:</strong> read by index O(1); insert/delete in middle O(n); search unsorted O(n).</li>
    <li><strong>Hash table:</strong> insert/lookup/delete O(1) average; O(n) worst case (hash collisions — the denial-of-service angle of bad hashing).</li>
    <li><strong>Balanced tree:</strong> O(log n) everything, and sorted iteration for free — why databases use B-trees on disk.</li>
    <li><strong>Graph:</strong> nodes + arbitrary connections — the general case. Social networks, maps, the web itself. Traversal (BFS/DFS) is the gateway algorithm (see the graphs lesson on this ladder).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The deeper skill</h2>
  <p>
    Real programs compose structures: a cache is a hash table plus a queue; a text editor is
    arrays of lines plus trees for undo. The skill is naming the access pattern first — what gets
    looked up, how often, in what order — then picking the least exotic structure that makes the
    common case cheap.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms; Sedgewick & Wayne, Algorithms, 4th ed.</p>
</TopicPage>
